{
  "case_id": "2008-08-305",
  "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:14 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gary S. Feinerman",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEINERMAN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The 1997 amendments to IDEA prohibit tuition reimbursement awards for students who are unilaterally placed in private school without first having received special education services from the public school district."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Feinerman, but the -- the first provision that you read, sub (i), says that there will be no reimbursement if the agency has made a free appropriate public education available to the child and the parents elected to place the child in private school."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gary S. Feinerman",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEINERMAN: Well, it's -- the tuition is $5,200 a month."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: But you -- you don't think -- you don't think a court could say the only reason these people could not comply with (ii) is that the school district made it impossible by not complying with the order to provide an IEP; and, therefore, they -- they can recover for the private placement?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Feinerman",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEINERMAN: That's the scheme that Congress wrote in the statute, yes, under -- under (C)(ii). And it has to work that way, because -- I want to come back to a -- to a point that was aired in the briefs, which is T.A.'s argument and the government's argument rests upon the notion that section 1415(i)(2)(B)(iii) gives hearing officers the authority to award tuition reimbursement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: How have -- how have hearing officers been proceeding? I mean, here we had a case that went to a hearing officer. There is no IEP when the hearing officer begins the process."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David B. Salmons",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, the ten-day period that is referenced in subsection (C)(iii) refers to the amount of notice that a district needs to receive before a child is removed from the public school system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David B. Salmons",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALMONS: Well -- well, if I -- if I may, the way we read this provision, and it's set forth on pages 3 and 4 on the addendum to the blue brief, and we think it's important that we read this as a whole and in context, is that subsection (C)(i) provides the general rule to govern the payment of private school tuition based on a unilateral placement by the parents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So you must find both, not just that the private school was a good place, but that the school district did not offer an adequate education for the child."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Salmon",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SALMONS: That is -- that is correct. That is what this Court held in Carter. That's what the regulations say. I would note that there is nothing in the text of the statute and the provisions that Petitioner relies on, (C)(ii) for example, that makes it a statutory requirement to show that the private placement is reasonable as a -- as a condition precedent to obtain tuition reimbursement. But both sides concede that that's required, even though it's not in the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0002-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MILLER: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Had Petitioner provided T.A. a special education that was later determined to be inadequate, there would be no question that the district court would have authority and discretion to determine whether an award of private school tuition reimbursement was appropriate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ERIC D. MILLER ON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0002-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MILLER: I think of the additional costs of treating special education children, above those of educating other children, I think approximately 10 percent, 10 to 12 percent, is reimbursed by the Federal Government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Where are you reading this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Feinerman",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEINERMAN: This isn't -- this regulation was brought up at argument. It's not in any of the addendums, but I -- I would direct the Court's attention to subsection (c) of 300.148. In terms of the -- the provision that T.A.'s lawyer addressed, 1415(b)(6), no argument -- there's no argument in the briefs that 1415(b)(6) gives hearing officers the authority to grant tuition reimbursement in these circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, what happens -- what happens if the hearing officer can't, but why couldn't the court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress got specific in 1997. There's a heading -- enacted a provision under the heading Reimbursement for Private School Placement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "it is equitable to pay the -- the reimbursement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-305",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "870bb9c4443f6cd9786e670380f878b72312f4c51f783b004e2b6a5cc9f9ea7e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-870bb9c4443f-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
